     5	"""
     6	Design analysis for the combo variant.
     7	
     8	a) Concrete behavior and trace
     9	- The visible ingredients stay close to the source task: blue endpoint pairs define 45-degree
    10	  segments, and magenta trigger pixels can lie on those segments.
    11	- Original task: fill the blue diagonal, then let every triggered magenta ray continue along the
    12	  perpendicular diagonal until it hits the current border.
    13	- Combo task: still fill the same blue diagonal, but every triggered magenta ray now has a fixed
    14	  length equal to floor(blue endpoint distance / 2). After drawing those finite rays in absolute
    15	  coordinates, enlarge the output canvas if needed so the whole drawing fits.
    16	- Example: blue endpoints at (2,2) and (10,10) give segment length 8, so every trigger on that
    17	  segment emits 4 magenta cells in each perpendicular direction. A trigger at (3,3) and another
    18	  at (7,7) both emit length 4 even though their distances to the nearest endpoint differ; this is
    19	  the key difference from the pure D3 variant. If one ray reaches row -1 or col 13, the output is
    20	  expanded with background rows or columns so those coordinates become visible.
    21	
    22	b) Complexity calibration
    23	- Only one numeric code is introduced: ray_len = floor(segment_len / 2). That keeps the rule
    24	  inferable from a few examples.
    25	- To make the distance code observable, the generator always uses two intended segments with
    26	  different segment lengths, so at least two ray-length tiers appear in one sample.
    27	- To separate this combo from the trigger-local D3 rule, one segment always carries two triggers
    28	  at different positions but with the same ray length.
    29	
    30	c) Implicit-assumption review of the original main()
    31	- `out = grid.copy()` is no longer sufficient because legal output cells may lie outside the input
    32	  rectangle. The combo solver paints in absolute coordinates and materializes the expanded canvas
    33	  afterward.
    34	- The original trigger collector stored only directions because the border decided the stop point.
    35	  The combo solver must store a per-direction finite reach.
    36	- The original while-loop stop condition was the current border. That assumption is invalid once
    37	  the true stop condition is the encoded reach and the border is derived afterward.
    38	- The original longest same-color diagonal-pair heuristic for identifying blue is preserved, but
    39	  the generator must prevent magenta distractors from creating a longer same-color diagonal pair.
    40	- Grouping blue points by diagonal key and connecting each group's extremes still assumes at most
    41	  one intended segment per diagonal family key; the generator enforces unique diagonal ids.
    42	
    43	d) Placement legality
    44	- Generated input pixels never overlap one another.
    45	- Output-only blue fills and full finite magenta rays are reserved in one absolute coordinate
    46	  system, so later segments cannot collide with already planned geometry even outside the input.
    47	- Distractors are placed only on cells absent from both the input occupancy set and the reserved
    48	  output set.
    49	
    50	e) Visual observability
    51	- Every generated sample has at least one true expansion event, so the larger output shape is not
    52	  optional.
    53	- Every generated sample has at least two distinct encoded ray lengths, so longer blue gaps visibly
    54	  lead to longer magenta extensions.
    55	- Every generated sample differs from the nearest-endpoint D3 baseline because one segment has two
